;; THE REPL SESSION — a mini workflow with an entry and an exit condition.
;;
;; orient -> declare -> explore -> land, and a branch cannot skip a step:
;; `eval` is refused until it has declared (phases.edn :repl-needs-a-plan) and
;; `done` is refused until it has landed (:plan-not-landed). Those two
;; refusals are the enforcement; THIS file is the shape, and the shape is what
;; an agent may rewrite.
;;
;; The failure it was built after (run bd56a286, GLM-5.3): 238 turns in the
;; REPL chasing a defect that was in the branch's own tests, because nothing
;; ever made it say where it thought the defect was; and a sibling branch that
;; read for 316 turns and wrote nothing. Neither responded to steering —
;; suggestion-only gates are 0-for-6 across two model tiers (karamazov-qic).
;; A declared file is a hypothesis, and having to state one is the point.
;;
;; :declare loops back to :orient when nothing was named, so an empty plan
;; cannot open the REPL. :land loops back to itself while files are owed,
;; which is the exit condition holding.
{:description "The REPL session: orient, declare, explore, land. The declaration is made with the `plan` tool - plan({files: [...], tests: [...], goal: \"one line\"}) - and at least one file is required; an empty plan is refused. Entry and exit are enforced as phases.edn refusals, :repl-needs-a-plan (eval is withheld until a file is named) and :plan-not-landed (done is withheld while a declared file is unwritten), so exploration cannot start without a hypothesis or end without a file. Calling plan again REPLACES the declaration, which is how a branch corrects a wrong guess. Reorder or drop a step here and the next session takes the new shape; the two refusals live in phases.edn and are what actually bind."
 :input-schema
 ;; What a driver hands this manifest to start a run. mycelium checks it in
 ;; run-compiled BEFORE any cell runs, so a caller that builds the map wrongly
 ;; is told which key, instead of a nil surfacing several cells later.
 [:map [:branch :map] [:turn :int]]

 :cells {:start    :repl/orient
         :declare  :repl/declare
         :explore  :repl/explore
         :land     :repl/land}

 :edges {:start    :declare
         :declare  {:planned :explore
                    :empty   :start}
         :explore  :land
         :land     {:complete :end
                    :owing    :land}}

 :dispatches {:declare [[:planned {:repl/planned? true}]
                        [:empty   _]]
              :land    [[:complete {:repl/complete? true}]
                        [:owing    _]]}

 :invariants
 [{:type :must-precede :cell :declare :before :explore :enforced true
   :protects "The REPL never opens before a file has been named. This is the
              whole contract: exploring is cheap and a REPL session dies with
              the branch, so the only thing that makes it worth doing is
              knowing which file it is going to end in."}

  {:type :must-precede :cell :explore :before :land :enforced true
   :protects "Landing follows exploring rather than replacing it — the point
              is not to skip the REPL, it is to bound it at both ends."}]}
